//# #2745: a let-bound name inside the interpolation is read in ITS OWN scope.
//
// Codex, P1 round 3 on #2761, and both directions it named reproduced. The
// compound walk recursed into a let-form's body with the OUTER `var_types`, which
// was wrong twice over. Measured on `"\{ { .. } }"` -- the braced spelling, which
// is how a let reaches here, since a bare `let` inside the braces does not parse:
//
//   { let b = a; b }   with `a: T`   printed `260` -- `b` was unknown, so the
//                                    refusal declined on a value that IS erased
//   { let a = 1; a }   shadowing     REFUSED -- the stale outer `a: T` survived,
//                                    so a program rendering an Int was rejected
//
// The control is what makes the diagnosis exact rather than plausible:
// `{ let n = 5; n }`, a fresh name, printed `5` both before and after. So the
// cause was the binding, not the walk.
//
// An unknown value type must still SHADOW the outer one. `extend_var_types`
// returns the environment unchanged for None, which is exactly what left the
// stale entry in place; the binding is made to "" instead, which masks it and is
// not a formal, so the body reports no answer and the refusal declines -- the
// same fail-open the leaf takes for an operand it cannot type.
//
// Both green rows live in `fixtures/lambda_bound_toplevel_witness_test.vibe`: a
// false REFUSAL rejects a correct program, and this fixture would keep refusing
// either way, so only the green side can catch it.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    "\{ { let b = a; b } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
